  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Essential tremor (MDS 2018 criteria): bilateral upper-limb postural and kinetic tremor, right more than left, for at least three years, worse over six months, without head or voice tremor or other neurological signs; enhanced by alcohol rebound and caffeine; thyroid and metabolic causes excluded</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G25.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis. The shakiness of his signatures is documented as an interpretation (CLM-0006, R, possible); no tremor is documented in his lifetime, and the mercury hypothesis (CLM-0010) is disputed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Hazardous drinking (AUDIT 12; about 24 standard drinks a week with heavier evenings with friends), without an alcohol use disorder; raised GGT</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z72.89</td><td>S — Synthetic finding. The only historical mention of his drinking is a story written down decades after his death (CLM-0008, R); it is not treated as evidence of his habits.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection (common cold) with a low-grade fever, one week after starting propranolol; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent illness; it does not assert anything about the fever in John Ward&apos;s later story (CLM-0008, R).</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

